  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>first option</b> is correct: Using AWS CloudWatch Events (now part of EventBridge) to monitor for state changes in SMS jobs allows you to detect when a new AMI is created. You can then trigger a Lambda function to retrieve the AMI ID, launch a new EC2 instance using it, and integrate with Jenkins to start testing. This is the best solution, as it automates the entire workflow from AMI creation to test execution.</p><p>Option <i>\"Create a replication job in SMS that will automatically deploy an EC2 instance based on a launch template as soon as the job is done.\"</i> is incorrect because SMS can replicate VMs and create AMIs, but it does not natively launch new EC2 instances automatically upon job completion.</p><p>The choice <i>\"Configure SMS to notify an SNS topic containing the AMI ID.\"</i> does not fulfill the requirement either. While SMS notifications to SNS are possible, this setup alone does not automate EC2 instance launching or integration with Jenkins.</p><p>In <i>\"Use CloudWatch Events to track AMIs tagged with AWS:SMS, invoking Lambda to spin up an EC2 instance and report the instance’s ID to Jenkins.\"</i>, monitoring tags may not be reliable as tags are not always applied consistently. Monitoring direct job state changes is more robust and event-driven.</p><p>Finally, <i>\"Assign a Lambda function as an SNS subscriber, which will launch the EC2 instance after a notification.\"</i> is not sufficient because simply subscribing to SNS does not ensure you receive the necessary information to launch an EC2 instance. The process needs to extract the new AMI ID and coordinate with Jenkins, which is most effectively managed via CloudWatch Event and Lambda.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Use event-driven AWS architectures for automation tasks. Monitoring SMS job state changes ensures prompt response, and a Lambda function can orchestrate both launching resources and notifying other systems like Jenkins.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are migrating an on-premises VM to AWS using AWS Server Migration Service (SMS). Every time SMS finishes creating a new AMI from its replication job, you must automatically launch an EC2 instance with that AMI and invoke a Jenkins automation test. The EC2 instance will be terminated after the testing is complete. Which approach best enables the automatic launching of the EC2 instance for testing when a new AMI is created?</p>",
